1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:05,764
Welcome to Episode Four, subtopic seven point three: Kill Zones.

2
00:00:06,184 --> 00:00:13,000
Until now we have studied where price goes. Kill Zones add a second question: when do we pay closest attention?

3
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:21,508
We will define them, fix one reference schedule, convert it to Malaysia time correctly, and then look carefully at FCPO.

4
00:00:23,328 --> 00:00:31,944
An ICT Kill Zone is a time window, stated in New York local time, that the methodology highlights for monitoring price delivery.

5
00:00:32,364 --> 00:00:39,036
Time matters because participation changes through the day, as major financial centres open and close.

6
00:00:39,456 --> 00:00:44,520
Session opens can bring new orders, scheduled news and runs on liquidity.

7
00:00:44,940 --> 00:00:54,108
But a Kill Zone is not an official exchange trading period. Exchanges define trading hours; ICT educators define Kill Zones.

8
00:00:54,528 --> 00:00:59,904
It is also not a guaranteed opportunity. Nothing has to happen because a window has opened.

9
00:01:00,324 --> 00:01:05,892
And it is not a universal law of markets. It is a convention inside one methodology.

10
00:01:06,312 --> 00:01:11,688
Think of it this way: the clock tells you when to look. Price tells you what is actually happening.

11
00:01:13,508 --> 00:01:24,092
ICT educators and resources do not all use the same windows, so CMI fixes one reference schedule, in New York time, and uses it in every episode.

12
00:01:24,512 --> 00:01:28,808
The Asian Kill Zone runs from eight p m to midnight New York time.

13
00:01:29,228 --> 00:01:33,404
The London Kill Zone runs from two a m to five a m.

14
00:01:33,824 --> 00:01:40,136
The New York AM Kill Zone runs from seven to ten a m, around many US data releases.

15
00:01:40,556 --> 00:01:46,148
New York Lunch, from noon to one p m, is often a lower-participation period.

16
00:01:46,568 --> 00:01:50,696
And the New York PM window runs from one thirty to four p m.

17
00:01:51,116 --> 00:02:00,332
Some sources use variants, such as a later New York morning window. That is fine, as long as you state which definition you use and do not mix them.

18
00:02:02,152 --> 00:02:07,624
Here is the same schedule on a twenty-four hour axis, starting at six p m New York time.

19
00:02:08,044 --> 00:02:12,388
The Asian window comes first, from eight p m to midnight.

20
00:02:12,808 --> 00:02:15,544
Then London, from two to five in the morning.

21
00:02:15,964 --> 00:02:18,796
Then the New York morning, from seven to ten.

22
00:02:19,216 --> 00:02:22,168
Followed by lunch, and the New York afternoon.

23
00:02:22,588 --> 00:02:30,700
Note New York midnight. Many ICT traders use it as a daily reference, which we will return to in the Judas Swing episodes.

24
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:38,856
Malaysia Time is UTC plus eight all year. Malaysia does not observe daylight saving.

25
00:02:39,276 --> 00:02:49,308
New York Standard Time, EST, is UTC minus five. During EST, Malaysia is thirteen hours ahead of New York.

26
00:02:49,728 --> 00:02:58,656
New York Daylight Time, EDT, is UTC minus four. During EDT, Malaysia is twelve hours ahead.

27
00:02:59,076 --> 00:03:10,620
In twenty twenty-six, daylight time runs from Sunday the eighth of March to Sunday the first of November. In twenty twenty-seven, from the fourteenth of March to the seventh of November.

28
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:17,880
So on the fifteenth of January twenty twenty-six, two a m in New York is three p m in Malaysia.

29
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:26,076
But on the fifteenth of July, two a m in New York is two p m in Malaysia. Same Kill Zone, different Malaysia time.

30
00:03:26,496 --> 00:03:33,120
These dates come from the IANA time zone database. Always check the date before you convert.

31
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:38,852
Here are the five windows again. The top row is New York time.

32
00:03:39,272 --> 00:03:49,304
The middle row shows Malaysia time while New York is on daylight time. London becomes two to five p m, and the New York morning becomes seven to ten p m.

33
00:03:49,724 --> 00:04:02,036
The bottom row shows Malaysia time while New York is on standard time. Every window moves one hour later: London is three to six p m, and New York morning is eight to eleven p m.

34
00:04:02,456 --> 00:04:08,984
Notice that the Asian window, eight p m to midnight in New York, is the next morning in Malaysia.

35
00:04:09,404 --> 00:04:13,484
So never use one fixed Malaysia-time schedule all year round.

36
00:04:15,304 --> 00:04:22,528
One more detail that catches many traders. The United States and the United Kingdom change their clocks on different dates.

37
00:04:22,948 --> 00:04:28,156
Before the eighth of March, two a m in New York is seven a m in London.

38
00:04:28,576 --> 00:04:37,696
From the eighth to the twenty-eighth of March, New York has moved but London has not, so two a m New York is only six a m London.

39
00:04:38,116 --> 00:04:43,300
From late March, both are on summer time, and the usual relationship returns.

40
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:53,008
The same mismatch happens for one week in late October. Because Kill Zones are defined in New York time, check the London relationship in these weeks.

41
00:04:54,828 --> 00:05:03,324
Now let’s connect Kill Zones with Level Three liquidity. This is a thirty-minute chart in New York time. Price ranges during the Asian window.

42
00:05:03,744 --> 00:05:09,864
The Asian high and Asian low become reference levels, alongside the previous day high and low.

43
00:05:10,284 --> 00:05:11,532
London opens.

44
00:05:11,952 --> 00:05:17,880
In this example, price sweeps the Asian low during the London window, then reverses higher.

45
00:05:18,300 --> 00:05:23,724
London leaves its own high and low behind, which become references for New York.

46
00:05:24,144 --> 00:05:29,784
In the New York morning, price trades through the London high and runs toward the previous day high.

47
00:05:30,204 --> 00:05:38,532
It sweeps the previous day high, then pulls back. These are possible behaviours around session windows, not mandatory patterns.

48
00:05:40,352 --> 00:05:48,968
Several things can make a window busy: session opens and overlaps, higher participation, scheduled economic releases.

49
00:05:49,388 --> 00:05:53,228
And liquidity resting at obvious session highs and lows.

50
00:05:53,648 --> 00:06:00,440
Other things make it quiet: holidays, thin participation, or a market waiting for a later announcement.

51
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:05,732
Sometimes an instrument’s main activity simply happens at another time of day.

52
00:06:06,152 --> 00:06:13,016
So do not assume every Kill Zone produces high volatility. Different instruments have different activity profiles.

53
00:06:14,836 --> 00:06:22,276
This is extremely important. FCPO trades on Bursa Malaysia Derivatives, with its own official sessions.

54
00:06:22,696 --> 00:06:27,208
The morning session runs from ten thirty to twelve thirty, Malaysia time.

55
00:06:27,628 --> 00:06:31,276
The afternoon session runs from two thirty to six p m.

56
00:06:31,696 --> 00:06:38,152
And the T plus one after-hours session runs from nine to eleven thirty p m, Monday to Thursday.

57
00:06:38,572 --> 00:06:47,836
These hours come from Bursa’s published product information. Verify the current circular before each intake, because exchanges do revise hours.

58
00:06:48,256 --> 00:06:56,488
Notice that the afternoon session overlaps the London window on the clock. That does not make it a London Kill Zone for FCPO.

59
00:06:56,908 --> 00:07:06,148
FCPO responds to its own participants, to Dalian and CBOT vegetable-oil markets, to the ringgit, and to palm-oil data.

60
00:07:07,968 --> 00:07:14,520
So how should we study time in FCPO? First, use the official Bursa sessions as the base.

61
00:07:14,940 --> 00:07:20,124
Second, mark each session’s open, high and low as liquidity references.

62
00:07:20,544 --> 00:07:29,880
Third, log international influences: when Dalian and CBOT are trading, ringgit moves, and palm-oil data releases.

63
00:07:30,300 --> 00:07:36,684
Fourth, test before you rely on any time pattern. Record many sessions and then judge.

64
00:07:37,104 --> 00:07:45,888
Time-of-day behaviour may exist in FCPO, but Kill Zones must be adapted and independently tested, never applied mechanically.

65
00:07:47,708 --> 00:07:51,308
Common mistakes. Using outdated session times.

66
00:07:51,728 --> 00:07:56,888
Ignoring daylight saving, or confusing EST with EDT.

67
00:07:57,308 --> 00:08:00,380
Assuming every Kill Zone creates a setup.

68
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:04,136
Treating Kill Zones as official exchange periods.

69
00:08:04,556 --> 00:08:09,020
Applying forex session assumptions directly to FCPO.

70
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:12,224
Ignoring major economic announcements.

71
00:08:12,644 --> 00:08:16,220
And entering a trade only because a Kill Zone has started.

72
00:08:18,040 --> 00:08:27,424
To summarise. Kill Zones are ICT time windows defined in New York local time, and CMI uses one consistent reference schedule.

73
00:08:27,844 --> 00:08:35,620
Malaysia is thirteen hours ahead during EST and twelve hours ahead during EDT, so always check the date.

74
00:08:36,040 --> 00:08:40,120
Kill Zones are not exchange hours and they do not guarantee movement.

75
00:08:40,540 --> 00:08:48,508
For FCPO, start from Bursa’s official sessions and test any time-based idea on FCPO itself.

76
